<h1>About batch.recipes</h1>
<p><strong>batch.recipes is a free website that turns any recipe into a batch-cooking plan.</strong> Add a screenshot or the text of a recipe, pick how many meals you want, and it gives you a grocery list, a cook order and a fridge and freezer plan.</p>
<p>It's for people who cook once and eat all week: meal preppers, budget cooks and anyone who finds recipes on TikTok, Instagram Reels or food blogs.</p>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A scaled recipe</strong> for the number of meals you pick, in US cups, ounces and pounds.</li>
<li><strong>A grocery list in store order</strong>: produce, meat, dairy, canned goods, dry goods, spices. Tick items as you shop, or send the list to Reminders, Notes, Google Keep or any list app.</li>
<li><strong>A cook order</strong>, one step at a time, with the screen kept on.</li>
<li><strong>A storage plan</strong>: what goes in the freezer, what goes in the fridge and what to add fresh when you eat.</li>
<li><strong>Freezer labels</strong> to print on Avery 5160 sheets or save as an image.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How the scaling works</h2>
<p>It follows batch-cooking rules, not plain multiplication. Meat, vegetables, grains and broth scale straight up. Salt, spices and oil are eased back past double, so a big batch isn't over-seasoned. Fresh finishers like lime and herbs are kept for serving. Pots are sized so stews fill no more than three-quarters and dry rice no more than a third. Baking isn't eased back and gets a warning past double, because it doesn't scale well.</p>

<h2>Questions</h2>
<h3>Is it free?</h3>
<p>Yes. There's no account and no sign-up. Store links and ads keep it free.</p>
<h3>Does it work with TikTok and Instagram recipes?</h3>
<p>Yes. Screenshot the recipe and add the screenshots. An AI model reads the ingredients and steps. If it can't, your phone reads them instead.</p>
<h3>Where are my recipes saved?</h3>
<p>On your phone or computer, not on a server. You can back them up to a file and restore them on a new phone. Screenshots are read and not kept. More in <a href="privacy">Privacy</a>.</p>
<h3>Does it work on iPhone and Android?</h3>
<p>Yes, in the browser. You can add it to your Home Screen to use it like an app.</p>

